\b\fs32 Instructor Notes: Shop Management and Rotation\b0\fs22\par
\par
\i Introduction to Welding Technology (CTE 1042). Instructor copy. Not for student distribution.\i0\par
\par
\b Booth capacity and rotation\b0\par
\par
The lab has eight welding booths and three cutting bays against a roster of up to twenty-four students per period. Nobody has enough booths, so the rotation is the lesson plan as much as the content is.\par
\par
Run three groups. One group welds, one group works the written or layout task at the classroom tables, one group runs the maintenance rotation. Swap on a fixed timer at fifteen minutes, announced aloud, rather than when a group appears finished. A fixed timer is what stops the confident students absorbing all the booth time, which is the default outcome of an informal swap and is invisible until you count it.\par
\par
\b The safety gate is not waivable\b0\par
\par
No student welds or cuts before passing the written safety assessment for that unit at one hundred per cent. This is stated in the pacing guide and it is worth restating here because the pressure to bend it is real: a student who missed the assessment day, a student who is one item short, a parent evening coming up. Re-teach the item and re-assess the same period. That takes four minutes and it is the whole answer.\par
\par
Keep the passed assessments in the booth folder, not in a drawer. When an administrator or an insurer asks how you know a particular student was cleared for torch work, the answer should be a piece of paper with a date on it.\par
\par
\b Maintenance rotation\b0\par
\par
The third group is doing real work, not being parked. Assign one of:\par
\par
Booth housekeeping: sweep the booth, empty the stub can, coil leads off the floor, check the curtain for burn-through, wipe the helmet lens and replace the cover plate if it is pitted.\par
\par
Consumables check: count electrodes by type, note anything below a quarter box on the whiteboard, confirm the rod oven is at temperature and log the reading.\par
\par
Cylinder check: confirm every cylinder is chained upright, caps fitted where no regulator is present, and hydrostatic test dates in date. Report anything out of date rather than moving it.\par
\par
Rotate which task each group gets across the semester. Students who only ever sweep learn that maintenance is punishment, and students who only ever check cylinders never learn what a pitted cover plate does to your ability to see a puddle.\par
\par
\b The rod oven\b0\par
\par
E7018 lives in the oven at two hundred and fifty degrees F. Electrodes left out overnight go back in the oven, not into a holder. This is the single item most often skipped when a class is rushed, and its consequence is hydrogen cracking in work that has already been graded and taken home.\par
\par
Log the oven temperature daily during the consumables check. A failed oven element is silent and the electrodes look fine.\par
\par
\b Reading the class from the bead\b0\par
\par
Two failure patterns account for most of what you will see in Unit 1, and they are described by students in the same words.\par
\par
Amperage too low: the electrode sticks, the arc keeps dying, the bead sits high and narrow. Students say the arc keeps going out.\par
\par
Arc too long: the arc flutters, the bead is wide and porous, the sound is hollow. Students also say the arc keeps going out.\par
\par
The corrections are opposite. Do not diagnose from the description; look at the bead. If more than a third of the class misses the amperage item on the written assessment, run the deliberate demonstration before Day 3: a bead thirty amps low, a bead thirty amps high, a bead in range, and have them name each from the sound with their backs turned.\par
\par
\b Unit 2 pacing risk\b0\par
\par
Two days is tight for oxy-fuel. Assembly and leak testing genuinely consume most of Day 1, and cutting a straight line is harder than it looks to anyone who has not tried it. If the class is behind, cut the circle-template extension rather than the leak test. The leak test is the part that matters and it is the part that looks skippable.\par
\par
\b Known gaps in this unit, to address before next semester\b0\par
\par
Unit 2 has no vocabulary handout. Students meet kerf, neutral flame, carburising, oxidising, backfire and flashback in the same two days and have nothing to take away. Unit 1's handout is the model to copy.\par
\par
Unit 2 has no answer key for the written assessment. It is currently marked from the lesson plan, which works only because the same instructor wrote both and is a problem the moment anyone covers this class.\par
\par
Neither unit has an exit ticket. The verbal exit question in Unit 1 Day 1 works but leaves no record, so there is no evidence of who understood electrode selection before booth day and who was simply quiet.\par
\par
No student practice worksheet exists for either unit beyond the assessments themselves. Students who finish a booth rotation early and are not yet at criteria have nothing structured to do.\par
